        "id": 18529
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-03T17:24:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "😁\n\nSPIR-V codegen tacks on a `_sampler` and `_texture` suffix when decorating the argument but this doesn\u0027t have to stay consistent with that. My only worry is that the superscript \u0027s\u0027 and \u0027t\u0027 can appear a little too tiny to distinguish at small font sizes which could be an issue while debugging.",
      "revId": "b6cdd538055df78b695328ec154cdd644c8e7673",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1ea03039_e93491dd",
        "filename": "src/sksl/codegen/SkSLWGSLCodeGenerator.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 91,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-03T17:54:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We have subscripted numbers in SkRP output--have these been legible for you? This should be ~equivalent. I can change them both if you find them hard to read.\n\nFWIW, I think there is actually a bit of technical merit to using an out-of-band character that isn\u0027t available in base SkSL--it neatly solves the problem of the user creating a variable like `tex_sampler` with a name that overlaps ours. In practice we seem to get away with it in Metal, since runtime effects end up with all sorts of unique decorations on their variables regardless, but non-ASCII characters eliminate all doubt (since SkSL rejects non-ASCII).",
      "parentUuid": "7608f5f8_68ce20e2",
      "revId": "b6cdd538055df78b695328ec154cdd644c8e7673",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "54c44a12_7c542441",
        "filename": "src/sksl/codegen/SkSLWGSLCodeGenerator.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 91,
      "author": {
        "id": 18529
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-03T20:31:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e We have subscripted numbers in SkRP output--have these been legible for you? This should be ~equivalent. I can change them both if you find them hard to read.\n\u003e \n\nI hadn\u0027t noticed that about SkRP output! TBH legibility is not a concern for me personally on a high DPI screen (such as on my MacBook) but the 1080p monitor I have at home doesn\u0027t resolve the characters very well. Either way, I think it\u0027s OK to keep this as is.\n\n\u003e FWIW, I think there is actually a bit of technical merit to using an out-of-band character that isn\u0027t available in base SkSL--it neatly solves the problem of the user creating a variable like `tex_sampler` with a name that overlaps ours. In practice we seem to get away with it in Metal, since runtime effects end up with all sorts of unique decorations on their variables regardless, but non-ASCII characters eliminate all doubt (since SkSL rejects non-ASCII).\n\nI think this is a decent argument for keeping the decoration as unicode given that SkSL rejects non-ASCII.",
      "parentUuid": "1ea03039_e93491dd",
      "revId": "b6cdd538055df78b695328ec154cdd644c8e7673",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f6e5fb9a_83bb8ab8",
        "filename": "src/sksl/codegen/SkSLWGSLCodeGenerator.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 2718,
      "author": {
        "id": 18529
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-03T17:24:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is this for shared tests that are written like `layout(binding \u003d X) uniform sampler2D`? Ideally such a declaration should be disallowed (the SPIR-V codegen rejects that with a message like `SPIR-V dawn compatibility mode requires an explicit texture and sampler index` when using the DawnCompatMode setting).\n\nIt would be nice if this produced a similar error while authoring shaders. What do you think about adding a compiler setting to enable this fallback behavior in tests and adding some wgsl-specific tests that don\u0027t use the setting to demonstrate the error case?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2717,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 2718,
        "endChar": 81
      },
      "revId": "b6cdd538055df78b695328ec154cdd644c8e7673",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "00dc43df_496d9c3d",
        "filename": "src/sksl/codegen/SkSLWGSLCodeGenerator.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 2718,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-03T17:54:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Is this for shared tests that are written like layout(binding \u003d X) uniform sampler2D? \n\nYes.\n\n\u003e It would be nice if this produced a similar error while authoring shaders. What do you think about adding a compiler setting to enable this fallback behavior in tests and adding some wgsl-specific tests that don\u0027t use the setting to demonstrate the error case?\n\nI\u0027m not convinced there is a reason to gild this lily. If you actually try to use this shader in a draw, WGSL should report a very clear binding error. This isn\u0027t user-facing (runtime effects don\u0027t use bindings) and it doesn\u0027t seem like there\u0027s a way that someone could get this wrong in Graphite/Ganesh in a subtle way where a flag would help them. We should get an error about a bad binding either way.",
      "parentUuid": "f6e5fb9a_83bb8ab8",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2717,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 2718,
        "endChar": 81
      },
      "revId": "b6cdd538055df78b695328ec154cdd644c8e7673",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d195f13e_e4f77b48",
        "filename": "src/sksl/codegen/SkSLWGSLCodeGenerator.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 2718,
      "author": {
        "id": 18529
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-03T20:31:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That\u0027s fair. Let\u0027s not block this CL on this either way.\n\nP.S.:\n\nIt looks like assigning an index beyond 10001 fails shader compilation, which is good, but it\u0027s not caught by Dawn or WGSL. I modified one of the compute shader tests that I have locally running on Graphite\u0027s Dawn backend and I only got an error from the Metal compiler during pipeline creation:\n\n    Error Domain\u003dMTLLibraryErrorDomain Code\u003d3 \"program_source:19:132: error: \u0027buffer\u0027 attribute parameter is out of bounds: must be between 0 and 30\n    kernel void computeMain(uint3 sk_GlobalInvocationID [[thread_position_in_grid]], const device inputBlock\u0026 _anonInterface0 [[buffer(10001)]], device outputBlock\u0026 _anonInterface1 [[buffer(1)]]) {\n                                                                                                                                       ^\n                                                                                                                                   \nBecause I\u0027m supplying a pipeline layout, I would have expected Dawn to catch the layout mismatch rather than the driver. Dawn does catch mismatches between the layout and the bindings on the API side during command encoding, so I know the layout is correct. I think IWBN if this were caught earlier. I\u0027ll double check if we\u0027re not disabling shader validation or anything similar.",
      "parentUuid": "00dc43df_496d9c3d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2717,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 2718,
        "endChar": 81
      },
      "revId": "b6cdd538055df78b695328ec154cdd644c8e7673",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7ffb629e_0be9d4b8",
        "filename": "src/sksl/codegen/SkSLWGSLCodeGenerator.cpp",
